    "PREAMBLE": "State vs Sandeep And Anr on 14 August, 2024\nIn The Court Of Additional Sessions Judge (Ftc)-02,\n South District, Saket Courts\nPresided Over By : Sh. Vishal Pahuja\nCnr No. Dlst01-012247-2023\nSc No. 522/23\nState Vs. Sandeep\nFir No. 270/23\nPs: Malviya Nagar\nU/S: 498A/304B Ipc\nState\n Versus\nSandeep,\ns/o Late Krishan Pal,\nr/o T-265, F-7, Ground Floor,\nChirag Delhi,\nNew Delhi .... Accused\nDate Of Institution : 12.12.2017\nDate Of Judgment/ Order : 14.08.2024\nFinal Order : Acquitted\n Judgment\n1.\n",
    "facts": "On preliminary inquiry, it was found that the deceased namely Neha got married to accused Sandeep on 10.02.2022, so her death being unnatural within seven years of marriage was informed to the Executive Magistrate.\nThe Executive Magistrate concerned conducted proceedings U/s 176 Cr.P.C. and recorded the statement of father of the deceased.\nDuring the proceeding various documents/exhibits were seized by investigating agency and statement of witnesses were recorded.\nAfter conclusion of the investigation, the police filed the charge sheet against accused Sandeep u/s 498A/304B IPC before Ld. Metropolitan Magistrate concerned.\nDuring trial, supplementary charge sheet was filed regarding RFSL report.\nThereafter, the present matter was received by way of committal to the Court of Sessions on 12.07.2024.\n6.\n",
    "ANALYSIS": "Additional PP for the state and even during their cross examination both the aforesaid witnesses failed to substantiate the case of the prosecution and categorically denied the facts allegedly stated by them in their previous respective statements.\nBoth the witnesses were cross examined on behalf of the accused as well.\nDuring their cross examination, both the aforesaid witnesses admitted that the deceased never complained with respect to demand of dowry by the accused at any point of time after the marriage.\nAs such the deposition of material witnesses PW1 and PW2 is of no help to the case of the prosecution.\n14. There is no independent public witness examined by the prosecution to establish its case.\nThere is not even iota of evidence adduced on record attributing the culpability of the accused in this case.\nIn absence of any cogent evidence lead on record by the prosecution, it can safely be concluded that the prosecution has failed to prove the guilt of the accused for the offences charged against him.\n",
    "decision": "Accordingly, accused Sandeep stands acquitted of the charges levelled against him in the present case.\n"
